---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { href, CONTACT_LINKS } from '../lib/site';

/*
 * SOURCE TRACE (Site build 3, back-propagation round, 2026-08-26)
 *
 * Body prose: `notes/career-narrative-draft.md` §2 at v4, the back-propagated
 * canonical narrative, carried near-verbatim. v4 folds the corrections the
 * LinkedIn About accepted across rounds 4-12 back into the canon; the
 * change-by-change record and its rulings are §10 of that file.
 *
 * What changed against the v3-based page:
 *   - Career proof moved up to the second paragraph ("I came up through the
 *     whole job"), per round 4 finding 2 and round 7 finding 1.
 *   - "Those are usually two people." cut, per the round 8a ruling.
 *   - The research-process paragraph loses its mechanism-detail sentence,
 *     per round 7 finding 2; "The first thing it caught was me" survives.
 *   - Layoff telling is now Variant C, the ruled compression. The Variant A
 *     licence for this surface is amended by §10, pending operator sign-off.
 *   - "So that's what I'm doing with the time" replaced by the six-month
 *     window through February 2027, per round 7 finding 3 (v4.1-signed form).
 *
 * Site-only additions, unchanged in role: the Standards pointer sentence, the
 * Field Guide link sentence, the Writing pointer, and the contact sentence
 * that renders only once CONTACT_LINKS has entries.
 *
 * Voice: zero em-dashes.
 */
---

<Base
  title="About"
  description="The career behind the question: Micron, Tableau, Comscore, and why this work has the shape it does."
  ogSlug="about"
>
  <div class="page-head">
    <h1>About</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Engineering executive and practicing architect. I work out what changes
      in a software organization when AI becomes part of the system, and I
      show the evidence.
    </p>
    <hr class="page-head__rule" />
  </div>

  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      If you run an engineering organization right now, you are being asked to
      make decisions about AI faster than anyone can hand you evidence for
      them. Ship it, don't ship it, restructure the team around it, tell your
      board what it's worth. That's the problem I work on, and I don't have a
      tidy answer to sell you.
    </p>

    <p>
      I came up through the whole job. I started in electrical engineering at
      Micron, on flash reliability, and my first boss out of school gave me
      the thing I still run on: trust your process, verify the output. Eight
      years at Tableau took me from tier four support through software
      engineering, product, and program management to director-level
      architecture. At Comscore I ran enterprise architecture, picked up
      technical strategy for a technology org of 200-plus when our CTO left
      in 2024, and finished as SVP Engineering with a direct org of about
      ninety.
    </p>

    <p>
      I'm an engineering executive and a practicing enterprise architect, and
      doing both at once is why the questions I care about sit across the
      line. How work should decompose between humans, models, and the systems
      that check them is a technical architecture question and an
      organizational one at the same time, and you can't answer half of it
      and call it done.
    </p>

    <p>
      The work started inside a company, not as a writing project. More than
      half my engineers weren't yet fluent enough in these systems to design
      around them responsibly, and I wasn't willing to ask them to build on
      something whose failure modes I couldn't explain. So I went looking for
      what we actually know, and most of what I found was a small number of
      claims echoing loudly. I ended up building a research process before I
      had anything to say. The first thing it caught was me, commissioning my
      own conclusion. That's the reason I trust it at all, and it's still an
      instrument, not an authority. What it produces, and the rules it is
      held to, are on <a href={href('/standards')}>Standards</a>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Late this July I wrote a field guide for the architects I lead. They'd
      noticed I kept deploying the same eighteen sayings and asked what was
      actually underneath them, so I wrote the reasoning down, with the prior
      art collected in an appendix because standing on somebody's shoulders is
      a feature. The stated goal of the document was that they could
      eventually operate the way I do without me in the room. That's the job
      as I understand it. A system that works in spite of me, not because of
      me. It is on this site, unchanged, as the{' '}
      <a href={href('/field-guide')}>Field Guide</a>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Two weeks later I was laid off. Second time in under four years. Tableau
      in January 2023, Comscore on August eleventh. I'm not going to make
      either one mean more than it does.
    </p>

    <p>
      My last charge was the course from a legacy data estate to an
      architecture that could carry the business into its next decade. I
      believed a data mesh could do it if we solved the governance problem. We
      solved neither before my tenure ended. The diagnosis is still untested,
      and that stings. It is also the reason this work exists in the shape it
      does: I got to observe and teach, and I did not get to enact and
      measure.
    </p>

    <p>
      I'm taking six months, through February 2027, to make the internal work
      public: the question, what the evidence supports, what it doesn't
      cover, and the decision I'd make anyway. Every number and every
      attribution carries a named source and a live link, or it comes out of
      the text. I'd rather publish nothing than add to the noise.
    </p>

    <p>
      If you're making these calls too, I want to hear where your experience
      doesn't match mine. Feedback is a gift and that's exactly the kind I'm
      looking for. The pieces are on <a href={href('/writing')}>Writing</a>.
      {
        CONTACT_LINKS.length > 0 && (
          <>
            {' '}The fastest way to reach me is{' '}
            {CONTACT_LINKS.map((link, i) => (
              <>
                {i > 0 && ' or '}
                <a href={link.href} rel="me noopener">
                  {link.label}
                </a>
              </>
            ))}
            .
          </>
        )
      }
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
